Briefly: this is a bank customer churn dataset with 10,000 rows and 14 columns (no missing values). It records customer demographics and account attributes and includes a churn label.

Columns and what they represent:
- RowNumber — row index / ordering (identifier)  
- CustomerId — customer identifier  
- Surname — customer last name (identifier)  
- CreditScore — numeric credit score  
- Geography — customer country/region  
- Gender — Male/Female  
- Age — years  
- Tenure — number of years the customer has been with the bank  
- Balance — account balance (float)  
- NumOfProducts — number of bank products used  
- HasCrCard — 1/0 indicating whether customer has a credit card  
- IsActiveMember — 1/0 indicating active membership  
- EstimatedSalary — estimated annual salary (float)  
- Exited — target variable (1 = customer left / churned, 0 = stayed)

Useful next steps (typical analyses):
- Check churn rate (Exited.value_counts / mean) and class balance.  
- Explore churn by Geography, Gender, Age, Tenure, Balance, CreditScore.  
- Preprocess: drop identifiers (RowNumber, CustomerId, Surname), encode Geography/Gender, scale numeric features.  
- Build a classification model to predict Exited; evaluate with ROC/AUC and confusion matrix.  

